21:20:47 | INFO | 🤖 Target: That is actually a really perfect comparison. Fast food and package delivery share a lot of the same characteristics, and the way we as customers experience them is very similar. In both cases, you have a job that looks incredibly simple on paper: put the burger in the bag, or put the box on the porch. Because the core task is so basic, it is completely understandable why people assume that mistakes only happen if the worker just isn't thinking. But if you look at how those jobs are actually structured by the corporations that run them, the real issue isn't that the jobs require *no* thinking—it's that the system doesn't give the workers *time* to think. Here is why those two industries produce so many visible mistakes: **1. Speed Over Accuracy** In fast food, workers are often graded by a drive-thru timer. They have literally seconds to listen to a crackly speaker, punch in modifications, grab the items, and hand them out. In delivery, drivers are given algorithms that calculate their routes down to the minute, often requiring them to drop a package every two or three minutes for hours on end. When corporations prioritize absolute, breakneck speed over everything else, human error skyrockets. **2. Cognitive Overload** It might not be "intellectual" work, but it is highly demanding on a person's working memory. A fast-food worker might be listening to an order in a headset while simultaneously counting out change for the window and bagging a third order. A delivery driver is watching traffic, listening to a GPS, and looking for a house number in the dark. Even a literal rocket scientist is going to mess up a fry order or miss a driveway if they are forced to multitask at that speed for eight hours straight. **3. Treating Humans Like Robots** The companies that run these services—whether it's Amazon or McDonald's—have designed these systems as if they are being operated by robots. But they are operated by humans. Humans get tired, they get distracted, they misread a label, or they just zone out after doing the exact same repetitive motion for the 400th time that day. So, your analogy is spot-on. We aren't shocked when a fast-food order is wrong, just like we aren't shocked when a package is misdelivered. But the root cause usually isn't that the worker is incapable of basic thought. It’s that they are working in a high-turnover, high-stress machine that practically guarantees mistakes will happen. As the customer at the end of that chain, you are the one who has to deal with the frustrating results of that business model. And it is completely fair to be annoyed when you just want the service you paid for to go smoothly.
